# DECLARED COUNT of occurrences of a pwg-branded ontology identifier.
# See scripts/verify_no_foreign_ontology_namespace.sh for why this exists.
#
# THE BASIS OF THIS NUMBER CHANGED TWICE, and saying so is the whole point of
# this comment. A ratchet number that silently changes basis is not evidence
# about anything: comparing it to the previous number compares two different
# questions and reads as a regression, or a win, that never happened.
#
#   BASIS 1   276, counting the unregistered domain only, excluding nothing.
#
#   BASIS 2   294, measured 2026-08-17 on 7cc2a6f, counting BOTH pwg-branded
#             domains and excluding the gate's own three files:
#                 unregistered domain    278
#                 mDNS-reserved domain    16
#
#   BASIS 3   0, measured 2026-08-20. The regex now also matches `urn:pwg:`,
#             and the migration ran. Both halves of that sentence matter and
#             the order is the uncomfortable part:
#
#                 what basis 2 could see        294
#                 what it could not see         192   (urn:pwg: in this tree)
#                 -------------------------------------
#                 real total before migration   486
#                 after migration                 0
#
# THE NUMBER WENT UP BEFORE IT WENT TO ZERO, AND THAT IS THE HONEST STORY.
# Basis 2 was not 294 out of 294. It was 294 out of 486, because a pattern
# built around a dotted host cannot match a URN. Had the migration run against
# basis 2 alone, this file would have read 0, the gate would have gone hard,
# and 192 pwg-branded identifiers would have shipped in the customer's primary
# keys with a green gate over them. That is the exact failure this file's
# BASIS 2 comment was written to prevent, one regex-shape away.
#
# ZERO IS NOT A TARGET THAT WAS REACHED, IT IS NOW A FLOOR. The gate is HARD
# at zero: it exits 1 on any occurrence, in any file, in any tree it is run
# against. There is no advisory state left to fall back into. If a re-vendor
# or a new dependency reintroduces one, the build fails, which is the point.
#
# THE EXCLUSIONS EXIST BECAUSE THE INSTRUMENT MUST NOT MEASURE ITSELF. The
# gate, this file and the gate's test all NAME the identifiers in prose in
# order to explain what they are for. Two documentation files that also named
# them -- docs/EGRESS_INVENTORY.md and cuts/DEFECTS_ROLLFORWARD.md -- were
# REWRITTEN rather than added to the exclusion list, deliberately: every new
# exclusion is a place a real occurrence can hide later, and the exclusion
# list should only ever hold files whose job is to police the thing.
0
